# For docker-compose.prod.yml, on the VM. Copy it to .env there (it is ignored by git). It has only
# what is NOT secret: the passwords are in the OCI Vault (secrets aireuskadi-postgres-password and
# aireuskadi-reader-password). deploy/fetch-secrets.sh reads them with the identity of the VM and writes
# them in /run/aireuskadi/secrets.env (memory), and deploy/aireuskadi.service gives both files to docker
# compose. Nobody types or copies a password.
POSTGRES_DB=aireuskadi
POSTGRES_USER=aireuskadi
# The read-only role of the services that only read.
POSTGRES_READER_USER=aireuskadi_reader
# Only for a try WITHOUT the Vault (never on the VM): the two passwords here, of letters, digits, "-"
# and "_" only (both go inside a URL; a "/", "+", "@" or ":" breaks it) and not the ones of the laptop.
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# POSTGRES_READER_PASSWORD=

# Where the images are, and which one. :pro is what deploy-pro.yml publishes.
GHCR_OWNER=asieramigorena
IMAGE_TAG=pro

# Only if the packages of the images are private. The folder with the config.json that
# `docker login ghcr.io` wrote (Watchtower reads the token of GHCR from it). It is /root/.docker if the login was done as root; with another user (ubuntu, for
# example) it is that user's ~/.docker. It does not work if config.json has a "credsStore" and no
# "auths" entry: log in with a plain config.
DOCKER_CONFIG_DIR=/root/.docker

# The tunnel is optional. To turn it on, uncomment this line.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=tunnel
# The Tailscale auth key of the tunnel (Settings -> Keys, not reusable, tag:aireuskadi) is a secret too:
# it is the secret aireuskadi-tailscale-authkey of the Vault, and fetch-secrets.sh puts it in
# TS_AUTHKEY. It is only read the first time: afterwards the identity is in the tailscale-state
# volume. Without that secret the stack starts without the tunnel key.
# For a try WITHOUT the Vault (never on the VM): TS_AUTHKEY=...
